   "text": {
    "L1": "Economic activities are grouped into three sectors: primary, secondary and tertiary.",
    "L2": "Primary activities take things directly from nature, such as farming, fishing and mining.",
    "L3": "Secondary activities turn raw materials into goods, such as bread, cloth or furniture.",
    "L4": "Tertiary activities provide services, such as transport, shops, banking and teaching.",
    "L5": "See how cotton moves through the sectors: a farm grows it, a mill makes cloth, and a shop sells it.",
    "L6": "The sectors depend on one another. If a harvest is poor, mills have less raw material and shops may have costlier goods.",
    "L7": "Here is a common myth: using machines makes mining a secondary activity.",
    "L8": "That is not true. Mining is primary because it takes minerals directly from nature; machines do not change its sector.",
    "L9": "Now classify this example: a fisherman."
   },
